# Every base image this repository builds from, pinned by digest, and every
# toolchain it installs, pinned by version and source hash. This file is the
# upgrade interface: change a value here, run `make build-env`, read what it
# says. Nothing else in os/build-env/ carries an image name or a version.
# The images are self-built (base + toolchain) rather than taken from `golang:`
# / `rust:`, so the Debian release, the CA bundle and git are this tree's
# choices rather than an upstream image's. The cost is that toolchain security
# updates arrive by editing this file.

# The pins are digests, not tags: `debian:trixie-slim` is a name upstream
# re-points on every rebuild, `debian:trixie-slim@sha256:...` is the bytes. Pin
# the index digest, not a per-architecture manifest digest: `docker buildx
# imagetools inspect <ref>` prints one `Digest:` for the whole
# multi-architecture index and then one per platform, and a per-architecture
# digest builds on the host that recorded it and refuses every cross build with
# a platform error.

# Sourced, not parsed. os/build-env/build.sh reads this with `.`, and the
# Dockerfiles read a comment-stripped derivative (`images.lock`) the same way.
# `${...}` on the right-hand side expands, so a value must be safe to eval:
# keep to plain assignments.

# To bump a digest: set the digest to the literal string PENDING -- keeping the
# `name:tag@` part -- and run `make build-env`. The build resolves the tag,
# prints the digest it found, and fails. Paste it in and run again. PENDING is
# a hard failure here with no environment variable that softens it: an unpinned
# base image fails nothing at all, ever, so a warning would float unread.

# A digest pin covers the base image's filesystem, NOT the apt archive the
# build installs from on top of it: `apt-get install git` against a frozen
# debian:trixie-slim still resolves against live deb.debian.org. The floor
# minimums below are what stands between this build and a silent toolchain
# downgrade, and mos-build-base records the versions it actually resolved
# (os/build-env/base/Dockerfile).

# Naming:
#   IMAGE_<distro>_<release>   an upstream base image, `name:tag@sha256:...`
#   <IMAGE>_FLOOR_<tool>_MIN   the minimum version a mos-build-<image> asserts
#   <IMAGE>_<tool>_VERSION     a toolchain tarball's version
#   <IMAGE>_<tool>_SHA256      that tarball's source hash
# The prefix before the first underscore is the consumer, and that is
# load-bearing: os/build-env/build.sh gives each image a lock file filtered to
# its own prefix, so adding a pin for one image does not invalidate the cached
# layers of the others.

# Base images.

# The floor for everything mos builds itself. os/rootfs/stages/10-base.Dockerfile
# assembles the device root from trixie, so a builder on any other release
# would link against a different glibc, libseccomp and libsubid than the one
# the binaries run on.
IMAGE_DEBIAN_TRIXIE=debian:trixie-slim@sha256:d7e12182ce18b85b93007c1dedf31f2d29e01ccf3182cc4017c709b6259bc132

# The pack stage of os/rootfs/stages/90-pack.Dockerfile and the three
# containers in os/tests/repart-loader-test.sh. One release behind the rootfs
# on purpose: the packer runs squashfs-tools, cryptsetup and libcap2-bin over
# an already assembled root, so moving it forward is a change to the packing
# tools rather than to what is packed. IMAGE_ keys are one per upstream
# reference, not one per consumer, which is why these two consumers share a
# key -- it is not a decision that the repart test tracks the packer.
IMAGE_DEBIAN_BOOKWORM=debian:bookworm-slim@sha256:88200866dfff7ea7f5cbcb6ec7c8a701889efe6fe859fe64d6990e4b07ea4171

# The BSP builders (os/boards/cx3576/bsp/{uboot,kernel}/Dockerfile,
# os/boards/cx3576/bsp/Dockerfile.alpine, the pack stage of
# os/boards/cx3576/bsp/rootfs/alpine/Dockerfile) and os/tests/handshake-test. Ubuntu
# and not trixie: these cross-compile a vendor kernel and a vendor U-Boot with
# gcc-aarch64-linux-gnu, python3-pyelftools, swig and device-tree-compiler at
# versions those vendor trees were tested against.
IMAGE_UBUNTU_2404=ubuntu:24.04@sha256:33ceb71981b602c1a7443a53469e4dba065f7503eab3078a2d7a57a2ab987517

# The rootfs stage of os/boards/cx3576/bsp/rootfs/alpine/Dockerfile -- the writable
# board-debug system, which is Alpine and not Debian for reasons recorded in
# os/boards/cx3576/bsp/rootfs/alpine/README.md.
IMAGE_ALPINE_3_24_1=alpine:3.24.1@sha256:28bd5fe8b56d1bd048e5babf5b10710ebe0bae67db86916198a6eec434943f8b

# The assembly and verification containers for cx3576, and the loop-device
# probe in .github/workflows/privileged.yml. os/build writes the GPT, the ESP
# and the filesystems of the shipped image from inside this base, and os/verify
# reads them back out of it: a container that partitions and formats what ships
# decides the on-disk bytes as much as one that compiles what ships.
#
# 3.21 and not IMAGE_ALPINE_3_24_1: these are two decisions that share a
# distro. 3.24.1 is the board-debug rootfs, a system that BOOTS; 3.21 is a
# toolbox that runs sgdisk and mke2fs and exits, chosen by the assembler's
# e2fsprogs floor (mke2fs >= 1.47, for `-O ^orphan_file` and `-E hash_seed`).
# It is an OCI index over amd64, arm64, arm, 386, ppc64le, riscv64 and s390x.
IMAGE_ALPINE_3_21=alpine:3.21@sha256:48b0309ca019d89d40f670aa1bc06e426dc0931948452e8491e3d65087abc07d

# The bun os/verify/run.sh and test/apid-api/run.sh run when the host has no
# bun binary, resolved with `os/build-env/from.sh --ref IMAGE_BUN_1`. CI takes
# that route: .github/workflows/check.yml installs no bun, so this pin is
# exercised on every push. MOS_APID_BUN_IMAGE still overrides for apid-api.
#
# NOT A DISTRO, which the key name should not hide. Every other IMAGE_ key here
# is <distro>_<release>; this one is <vendor product>_<major>.
IMAGE_BUN_1=oven/bun:1@sha256:5ff609364c049b54eb0ff560ec96319729a972078ef2c755d758f0c6ef89c2d6

# The docker client os/verify/run.sh layers onto IMAGE_BUN_1 via
# os/verify/Dockerfile whenever it has to run the full verifier (`--verify`,
# i.e. `make os-verify-<board>-v2`) on a host with no bun. The verifier drives
# docker itself -- it reads sgdisk, mtools, debugfs, unsquashfs and veritysetup
# out of IMAGE_ALPINE_3_21 -- and the bun image carries no client, so without
# this every route inside it ends at `docker: command not found`. Mounting the
# daemon socket does not help; what is missing is the client. Nothing else
# reads this key, and a host that has bun never builds that image at all.

# `28-cli` and not `docker:cli` or the full `docker:28`: `cli` is a floating
# major, `28-cli` is a floating patch that the digest nails to bytes, and the
# `-cli` variant carries the client and NOT dockerd. The client is a static
# binary, which is what lets an alpine-built client run in the debian-based bun
# image.

# The cost: running the full verifier on a bun-less host mounts
# /var/run/docker.sock into a container. Nested containers are siblings on the
# host daemon, not children -- which is why the identity mounts still resolve,
# and also why anything inside that container can do anything the host daemon
# can. It is an OCI index over amd64, arm64/v8, arm/v6 and arm/v7.
IMAGE_DOCKER_CLI_28=docker:28-cli@sha256:625d9431a9f54c5a2bc90f24f0e1c3d55b1349fd857dd85035f98c2c9acbdd4d

# The Dockerfile frontend -- the `# syntax=` line at the top of every
# Dockerfile in this tree. BuildKit reads `# syntax=<image>` before anything
# else and hands the whole Dockerfile to that image to parse: remote code,
# fetched at build time, with the power to decide what every instruction means.
# `docker/dockerfile:1` is a major-version channel upstream repoints on every
# 1.x release, and this repository signs update bundles that devices install.

# It is the one pin also written out at its call sites, because `# syntax=` is
# consumed before any ARG exists and cannot take a --build-arg. What makes
# those literals safe is that os/build-env/build.sh checks every one of them
# against this key and refuses a tree where any has drifted. An OCI index over
# twelve architectures.
IMAGE_DOCKERFILE_FRONTEND=docker/dockerfile:1@sha256:ecfaec9ed6d810b56388c508f4121597bfbba70d41a6dfeee4d8cad5f295fc32

# mos-build-base: the common floor -- the packages every builder needs
# regardless of language, and nothing else. Per-language apt lists stay in
# their own Dockerfiles, so a one-package edit does not recompile unrelated
# components.

# These are minimums, not pins, because the digest above does not pin the apt
# archive. What they catch is a floor tool that went missing or went backwards,
# which is what a base swap or a slimmed package looks like from inside the
# build; a trixie point release moving git forward passes, as it should. The
# values are trixie's: git 2.47.3, file 5.46, binutils 2.44, xz 5.8.1. Raise
# one only when trixie has actually moved, or the assertion stops meaning
# anything.
BASE_FLOOR_CODENAME=trixie
BASE_FLOOR_GIT_MIN=2.47
BASE_FLOOR_FILE_MIN=5.46
BASE_FLOOR_BINUTILS_MIN=2.44
BASE_FLOOR_XZ_MIN=5.8

# The images this repository builds, named for the FROM lines that consume
# them. Not an IMAGE_ key, deliberately: IMAGE_ means "an upstream reference,
# pinned by digest, and os/build-env/build.sh refuses it if it is not", while
# this is an image this repository built moments earlier in the same run. It
# has no digest that could be recorded here -- mos-build-base's id is not
# reproducible across a rebuild, because the apt layer is not and because
# buildx attaches a fresh provenance attestation even when every layer is
# cached.

# What pins the child images is the parent's own pin, one level up:
# mos-build-base is built from a digest, asserts its floor, and records what it
# resolved in /etc/mos-build/base.env. The chain is pinned at its root. The
# LOCAL_ prefix is load-bearing for the lock filter: no image row lists it, so
# these keys reach no image's images.lock and cannot invalidate anyone's
# layers. They travel as a --build-arg and nothing else.
LOCAL_MOS_BUILD_BASE=localhost/mos-build-base

# mos-build-base is the FROM of the other three, and these four are the FROM of
# every component build in the tree: os/pkgs/podman's src, c-build, rust-build,
# go-build and verify stages, os/pkgs/rauc's src and build stages, and
# os/pkgs/mosd/hack/build-target.sh's cross build. They are keys and not literals for
# the same reason every IMAGE_ line is: os/build-env/from.sh is the one place
# that answers "what does this build run on".

# On their own these pin nothing. They are local tags whose bytes are whatever
# `make build-env` last wrote, which is why os/build-env/from.sh refuses one
# that is not in the local image store rather than letting docker report it as
# a failed registry pull of a host called `localhost`.

# NO TAG on any of these four values, and that is the point rather than an
# omission. A local tag carries exactly one architecture, so the architecture
# is part of the name: os/build-env/build.sh writes
# `localhost/mos-build-c:${arch}` and os/build-env/from.sh appends the same
# `:${arch}` when a caller resolves the key with --arch. Both families coexist
# in one store, which is what the cx3576 image path needs -- it wants an amd64
# mos-build-rust for the mosd cross build in the same run that wants an arm64
# mos-build-c for rauc. Writing a tag into these values would put the
# architecture in two places and let them disagree. the record what the
# architecture-less name cost: two families destroyed each other on one host
# inside an hour, the loser being whichever built first.
LOCAL_MOS_BUILD_C=localhost/mos-build-c
LOCAL_MOS_BUILD_GO=localhost/mos-build-go
LOCAL_MOS_BUILD_RUST=localhost/mos-build-rust

# mos-build-c: the C/C++ toolchain.

# Floors, not exact versions, for the same reason BASE_FLOOR_* are floors:
# every one of these comes from `apt-get install` against live deb.debian.org,
# which the digest above does not pin. The Go and Rust blocks below are the
# opposite case -- a tarball pinned by sha256 is exact, and is asserted
# exactly. The values are trixie's under mos-build-c's own apt list: gcc
# 14.2.0, make 4.4.1, cmake 3.31.6, pkgconf 1.8.1, ccache 4.11.2. Raise one
# only when trixie has actually moved.

# autoconf, automake, libtoolize and python3 are asserted by presence and carry
# no floor: they are build-time drivers whose output is the project's own
# configure script, not libraries anything links against. Note libtoolize and
# not libtool -- the `libtool` package installs libtoolize, and the `libtool`
# command itself is generated per-project by configure, so a check for it fails
# on a correct image.
C_FLOOR_GCC_MIN=14.2
C_FLOOR_MAKE_MIN=4.4
C_FLOOR_CMAKE_MIN=3.31
C_FLOOR_PKGCONF_MIN=1.8
C_FLOOR_CCACHE_MIN=4.11

# mos-build-go: the Go toolchain, from the upstream tarball.

# 1.26.7 and not 1.27.0: Go supports the two most recent major lines, 1.26.7 is
# the latest patch of the mature one, and a build environment is the wrong
# place to be first.

# The URLs live here because images.env is sourced, and so is the
# comment-stripped lock derived from it, so ${GO_VERSION} expands in both. The
# version therefore appears exactly once in the tree. GO_VERSION must stay
# above the URLs that read it -- a sourced file is ordered.

# The hashes are resolved by os/build-env/build.sh and checked against
# upstream's own published checksums (go.dev/dl's release metadata; for Rust
# below, static.rust-lang.org's channel manifest). Both directions matter: the
# resolver proves the bytes this repository can fetch, the published checksum
# proves those bytes are the ones upstream signed off on.

# Nothing pins cross-compilation for Go, and that is a property of Go rather
# than an omission: the stock tarball cross-compiles to every GOOS/GOARCH it
# supports with no extra component and no cross linker, which is why there is
# no GO_STD_* block here and there is a RUST_STD_* block below. mos-build-go
# asserts that rather than assuming it.
GO_VERSION=1.26.7
GO_URL_AMD64=https://go.dev/dl/go${GO_VERSION}.linux-amd64.tar.gz
GO_URL_ARM64=https://go.dev/dl/go${GO_VERSION}.linux-arm64.tar.gz
GO_SHA256_AMD64=ffb5f8de10c62550dfddab66b36b57030721e0a44a3218e9e1181d7b59f121ca
GO_SHA256_ARM64=5a4ec883379d51ee9ce1040d5e87f8d35e20387574dd8c947feb01eabc3c1b37

# mos-build-rust: the Rust toolchain, from the upstream standalone installer.

# The current stable, where Go takes the mature line, because the policies
# differ: Go supports two major lines at once, Rust fixes only the current
# stable, so anything older is a line that receives nothing. os/pkgs/mosd/Cargo.toml
# declares rust-version = 1.96 and edition 2024, which 1.98.0 satisfies.

# The triple is a key because it is used twice -- once to build the URL and
# once to name the rust-std component install.sh has to be asked for. Written
# out twice it is a thing that can disagree with itself. The URLs are the
# undated permalinks, not the dated dist/<date>/ paths the channel manifest
# publishes: both serve identical bytes, and the undated form is derivable from
# RUST_VERSION.

# RUST_STD_* is the cross-compilation pin. The standalone `rust-` tarball ships
# std for its own host triple only, so a mos-build-rust built from it can build
# for exactly one target -- and the target this project ships to is the other
# one. The cross std is a separate upstream tarball, pinned here the same way,
# and the cross linker is an apt package asserted in the image.
RUST_VERSION=1.98.0
RUST_TRIPLE_AMD64=x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu
RUST_TRIPLE_ARM64=aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu
RUST_URL_AMD64=https://static.rust-lang.org/dist/rust-${RUST_VERSION}-${RUST_TRIPLE_AMD64}.tar.xz
RUST_URL_ARM64=https://static.rust-lang.org/dist/rust-${RUST_VERSION}-${RUST_TRIPLE_ARM64}.tar.xz
RUST_SHA256_AMD64=ed8ee2df70909c88cbaf87a6cfa3920dac00b537de12a6abe6906641e0f5952f
RUST_SHA256_ARM64=ac9283184301aeed06ecc9f5aa4c1be7041e18a1b197b6cb6c5d162d98f566da
RUST_STD_URL_AMD64=https://static.rust-lang.org/dist/rust-std-${RUST_VERSION}-${RUST_TRIPLE_AMD64}.tar.xz
RUST_STD_URL_ARM64=https://static.rust-lang.org/dist/rust-std-${RUST_VERSION}-${RUST_TRIPLE_ARM64}.tar.xz
RUST_STD_SHA256_AMD64=f5022e6c95a5ad23cca2513dc8281200f585fa188de6370aa37b128a43f876a3
RUST_STD_SHA256_ARM64=a36f7ac98af20ef0ba6368aace0345efabbebaef7962eba99f47190fd256162d
